# Cross-repo siblings cloned by setup-dev-env (`clone-siblings`).
#
# A BARE entry is cloned at the revision this commit's committed `repro.lock`
# pins for it (`lock-store: committed`, the action's default). That is the one
# place sibling revisions are recorded: `repro.lock` is what `repro build` /
# `repro test` resolve against, and a bare entry here makes the `nix`-flavor
# jobs in ci.yml build against exactly the same revisions.
#
# Do NOT add `=<sha>` to an entry the lock pins. An explicit revision that
# differs from the lock is an override: clone-siblings warns and clones the
# override, so CI tests one sibling set while `repro.lock` records another.
# Every entry here once carried such a SHA, each one older than the lock pin;
# `tests/test_render_native_launcher_entry.nim` does not compile against the
# isonim-tui revision named then (`cannot open file: isonim_tui/reactive_root`:
# the module is newer). To move a sibling, move the lock
# (`repro lock update <sibling>`), not this file.
isonim
isonim-android
isonim-cocoa
isonim-freya
isonim-gpui
isonim-render-serve
isonim-tui
isonim-tui-serve
nim-acp
nim-agent-harbor
nim-agents
nim-everywhere
nim-pty
nim-termctl
# `config.nims` puts these two on the import path for the transitive imports
# isonim re-exports, but no `uses:` edge in `repro.nim` names them, so the lock
# does not pin them. An explicit 40-hex commit is the clone-siblings form for a
# repo the lock has no opinion on (never a branch name: a branch tip is an
# unpinned build).
nim-faststreams=ce27581a3e881f782f482cb66dc5b07a02bd615e  # nim-faststreams@stable
nim-stew=83eb1157963b7f49351dbdd858355fa990bbe23c  # nim-stew@master
